## Rules

- Add a component with `solarian create-plugin <type> {{ short }}` (`actuator`, `sensor`, `module`, `robot`, `command`, `policy`, `op`). It writes the stub and merges the entry point; do not hand-write either.
- An entry point's class path must import. After changing one, reinstall (`pip install -e .`) so the metadata updates.
- Depend on other plugins instead of copying them: a module driven by Feetech servos depends on `solarian-feetech`.
- Anything outside Python files that must ship (yaml under `templates/`, the `agent/` folder, meshes, models) has to be listed in `[tool.setuptools.package-data]`.
- A skill never holds a `Module`. It reads `self.observations[name]` and returns `Action`s addressed to module names passed in `params`, so it runs on the robot or the controller alike.
- A module interprets actions; it has no methods that build actions for others. Named poses and task vocabulary belong to the skill's config, hardware limits to the module's.
- Keep `{{ package }}/agent/AGENTS.md` short and true: what the plugin provides, the class to import, the config it seeds, the mistake agents make with it.

## Check a change

```bash
pip install -e .                                   # metadata and entry points
python -c "import {{ package }}"                   # the package imports
solarian install /path/to/{{ name }}               # from a test project: seeds conf/, adds the dependency
```

Then exercise it in that project, in simulation first (`solarian run-robot --sim`).

